Time: O(m × n) 
Auxiliary space: O(m × n)

///we are using multi source BFS

class Solution {
    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {

        int m=grid.length;
        int n=grid[0].length;

        Queue<int[]> queue=new LinkedList<>();

        int fresh=0;
        int minutes=0;

//count fresh oranges and rotten oranges and add in queue
        for(int i=0;i<m;i++){
            for(int j=0;j<n;j++){

                if(grid[i][j]==2){
                    queue.offer(new int[]{i,j});
                }else if(grid[i][j]==1){
                    fresh++;
                }
            }
        }


        int[] dr={-1,1,0,0};
        int[] dc={0,0,-1,1};

        while(!queue.isEmpty() && fresh>0){
            
            int size=queue.size();

            for(int i=0;i<size;i++){

                int[] current=queue.poll();

                for(int d=0;d<4;d++){

                    int nr=current[0]+dr[d];
                    int nc=current[1]+dc[d];

                    if(nr>=0 && nc>=0 && nr<m && nc<n && grid[nr][nc]==1 ){
                        grid[nr][nc]=2;
                        fresh--;
                        queue.offer(new int[]{nr,nc});
                    }
                }
            }
            minutes++;
        }
        
        return fresh==0?minutes:-1;


    }
}


1. Initialize a queue, fresh = 0, and minutes = 0.
2. Traverse the grid, enqueue all rotten oranges, and count fresh oranges.
3. Define the four directions: up, down, left, and right.
4. While the queue is not empty and fresh oranges remain:- Store the current queue size.
  - Process all oranges at that BFS level.
  - For each orange, check its four neighbors.
  - If a neighbor is fresh, mark it rotten, decrement fresh, and enqueue it.
  - Increment minutes after processing the entire level.

5. Return minutes if fresh == 0; otherwise, return -1.
